This document describes ohow to configure and use the mppcreatecluster.sh script

Overview
--------

The mppcreatecluster.sh script creates a QD and a number of QE's which can be on
a single machine or multiple machines. Configuration is a three stage process:
1.	Update the cluster configuration file
2.	Select the machine/machines upon which the cluster will be built
3. 	Run the script ./mppcreatecluster.sh -c <cluster configuration filename>

The mppcreatecluster.sh script has one manadatory parameter [ -c <cluster configuration filename].
The cluster configuration file can be any name you choose, but must contain the expected
number of variables.

The Cluster Configuration File
------------------------------
CVS contains an example file cluster_example, which is configured for a single node QD/QE
environment.

The following provides an overview of each of the variables contained within the file.

CLUSTER_NAME="XXX YYY"	
			This sets the cluster name within the mpp.mpp table on the QD
			and QE's. This can be any text as required, if this variable
			is not set, the mppcreatecluster.sh script will set this to
			a default value of "MPP Cluster"

MACHINE_LIST_FILE=<name of file containing the machine hostnames to be configured as QE's
			Format for this file is
			hostname1
			hostname2
			hostname3
			..etc..
			If the required configuration is a single host with a QD and a
			number of QE's, just enter that hostname in the file.
			For a multi-host cluster, if the file contains the name of the
			QD host, QE's will be created on the QD host, if the QD hostname
			is not in this file, the QD will be created as a single instance
			with no QE's on that same host.

SEG_PREFIX="SSS"
			This variable sets the naming prefix for the QD and QE postmaster
			data directories.
			The mppcreatecluster.sh script will create a sub-directory named
			${SEG_PREFIX}0, the QE's will be named as follows (depending
			on the number of QE's being created)
			${SEG_PREFIX}1
			${SEG_PREFIX}2
			
PORT_BASE=nnnnn		
			This variable sets the port range for the QE postmasters.
			Where there are multiple QE's/host, ports will be assigned
			as nnnnnn+1, nnnnnn+2, nnnnnn+3 etc.
			Port numbers across QE's will be the same, so for example if
			there are 2 hosts, each with 2 QE's, ports will be
			host1		QE1	60001
			host1		QE2	60002
			host2		QE3	60001
			host2		QE4	60002
			It should be noted that the PORT_BASE should be set at a value
			that is not expected to clash with other ports that may already
			be assigned on a QD/QE.

declare -a DATA_DIRECTORY=(/dir1 /dir2 /dir3)
			This array determines the number of primary QE's that will be created
			on a host machine.
			In the above example, each machine will be configured with 3 QE's.
			The resulting data directory for each QE will be:
				/dir1/${SEG_PREFIX}[QE number]
				/dir2/${SEG_PREFIX}[QE number]
				/dir3/${SEG_PREFIX}[QE number]

QD_HOSTNAME="testhost"
			This variable sets the name on which the QD instance will be created, the
			mppcreatecluster.sh expects to be executed on this host, and will exit if
			this is not the case.

QD_DIRECTORY=/xxxx
			This sets where the QD data sub-directory will be created.

QD_PORT=nnnnnn		
			This sets a specific port number of the QD instance.

IP_ALLOW="XXX.YYY.ZZZ.AAA/CC"
			This is the value that will be added to each pg_hba.conf file
			to allow the QE's to connect to the QD instance.

TRUSTED_SHELL="ssh"
			This determines the mechanism that will be used to configure remote
			QE hosts.
DATABASE_NAME="dbname"
			if this variable is set, the mppcreatecluster.sh script will create
			the $DATABASE_NAME database when all QE's have been created. If this
			variable is not set, the mppcreatecluster.sh script will not create
			this database.

CHECK_POINT_SEGMENTS=X
			This value will ba added to each postgresql.conf file.

ENCODING=UNICODE
			This is used when each instance is initialized.

MIRROR_PORT_BASE=nnnnnn
			NOT CONFIGURED AT THIS TIME. DO NOT UNCOMMENT.

declare -a MIRROR_DATA_DIRECTORY=(/dir4 /dir5 /dir6)
			NOT CONFIGURED AT THIS TIME. DO NOT UNCOMMENT.

STANDBY_HOST=hostname
			NOT CONFIGURED AT THIS TIME. DO NOT UNCOMMENT.

STANDBY_IP_ALLOW="XXX.YYY.ZZZ.AAA/CC"
			NOT CONFIGURED AT THIS TIME. DO NOT UNCOMMENT.

Single Host Configuration Example
---------------------------------

The following variable settings will create a 3 QE cluster on a single host called testhost.
CLUSTER_NAME="Test Cluster"
MACHINE_LIST_FILE=hostlist #This file should contain "testhost"
SEG_PREFIX=testdb
PORT_BASE=45000
declare -a DATA_DIRECTORY=(/dbfast1 /dbfast2 /dbfast3)
QD_HOSTNAME=testhost
QD_DIRECTORY=/dbfast4
QD_PORT=5431
IP_ALLOW=<IP address of testhost"
TRUSTED_SHELL="ssh"
CHECK_POINT_SEGMENTS=8
ENCODING=UNICODE

If the above variables were contained in a file named cluster_test to create the cluster
issue the below command

$> ./mppcreatecluster.sh -c cluster_test


SCRIPT OVERVIEW
---------------
The following provides a functional overview of the mppcreatecluster.sh scripts.

1.	Checks for mpp_bash_functions.sh file, exits if unable to locate file.

2.	Sets script specific parameters.

3.	Checks if -c parameter supplied, if not calls USAGE function.

4.	Calls CHK_PARAMS function, which undertakes the following:
	a.	Checks if both MPPHOME and BIZHOME unset, if so, exits with suitable error
		message.
	b.	Checks to ensure that can find an initdb, if not, exits with suitable error
                message.
	c.	Checks to make sure that there is a valid cluster configuration file supplied
		to script with the -c paramater, if not, exits with suitable error
                message.
	d.	Sources the contents of the cluster configuration file.
	e.	Checks the host file named in the cluster configuration file, exits if issue
		with file with suitable error message.
	f.	Checks for duplicate hostnames in the host file, exits with suitable error
		message if any duplicate hostnames found.
	g.	Checks CLUSTER_NAME parameter, if unset, sets to a default value of "MPP Cluster"
	h.	Checks QD_HOSTNAME paramater, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	j.	Checks SEG_PREFIX paramater, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	k.	Checks QD_PORT, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	l.	Checks PORT_BASE, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	m.	Checks number of values in DATA_DIRECTORY, if zero exits with suitable error message.
	n.	Checks QD_DIRECTORY paramater, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	o.	Checks DATABASE_NAME, if unset, creates MPP cluster without creating the database
		name contained in DATABASE_NAME variable.
	p.	Checks IP_ALLOW, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	q.	Checks TRUSTED_SHELL, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	r.	Checks CHECK_POINT_SEGMENTS, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	s.	Checks ENCODING, if unset, exits with suitable error message.
	t.	Checks number of primary QE's, exits with suitable error message if zero.
	u.	If mirroring configured checks to ensure that the number of primary and mirror
		directories match, if not exits with suitable error message.

5.	Calls CREATE_QE_ARRAY function, which undertakes the following
	a.	Builds an array of primary QE's, with required/calculated paramaters
	b.	If mirroring configured, creates a mirror QE array.
	NOTE:-	For mirroring:
		If single host cluster, all mirrors created on that host.
		if multiple host, all primary QE instances for a single host, will be spread
		across the other hosts, so that no two mirrors from a host are located on another
		host where possible.

6.	Calls CHK_QES function, which undertakes the following
	a.	For each host, checks via ping, if any error, exits with suitable error message.
	b.	For each host, checks that data directories exist, if not exits with suitable error message.
	c.	For each host, checks to ensure that the intended QE instance directory does not exist, if
		it does exits with suitable error message.
	d.	If mirroring configured repeats directory check.

7.	Calls DISPLAY_CONFIG function, which undertakes the following
	a.	Displays QD details and other parameters passed or calculated by the script.
	b.	Displays a list of primary QE settings.
	c.	If mirroring configured, displays a list of mirror QE settings.	
	d.	Displays a continue prompt (If -a parameter not passed to script).

8.	Calls CREATE_QD_DB function, which undertakes the following
	a.	Checks to ensure that the intended QD instance directory does not exist, if so
		exist with suitable error message.
	b.	Calls initdb to create QD database, if error, exits with suitable error message.
	c.	Sets port# in QD postgresql.conf file, if error, exits with suitable error message.
	d.	If postgresql_conf_mpp_additions file exists, adds lines from this file to
		QD postgresql.conf file.
	f.	Adds IP_ALLOW value to QD pg_hba.conf file, if error, exits with suitable error message.
	g.	Starts QD instance, if error copies QD database log to script log file and exits
		with suitable error message..
	h.	Creates required backout commands.
	i.	Updates MPP system tables, with QD and QE details.

9.	Calls CREATE_QES_PRIMARY function, which undertakes the following
	a.	For each primary QE instance directory per host
		i)	Calls initdb, if error , exits with suitable error message. 
		ii)	Updates QE postgresql.conf file, if error, exits with suitable error message.
		iii)	If postgresql_conf_mpp_additions file exists, adds lines from that
			file to the QE postgresql.conf.
		iv)	Updates QE pg_hba.conf file, if error, exits with suitable error message.
		v)	Starts QE instance, if error, exits with suitable error message.
		vi)	Creates required backout commands (Note, this done at various stages
			throughout this fucntion).
		vii)	Updates QE mpp.mpp table.

10.	Calls CREATE_QES_MIRROR if mirroring configured, see 9. for details.

11.	If DATABASE_NAME variable set, creates the named database, if error, exits with 
	suitable error message.

12.	Calls RESTART_QD_PRODUCTION function, which undertakes the following
	a.	Calls mppstop.sh to stop the cluster, if error, exits with suitable error message.
	b.	Calls mppstart.sh to start the cluster, if error, exits with suitable error message.

13.	Displays useful information following cluster restart.

14.	Exits with zero.
